{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Cherry Blossoms and City Lights: My Week in Japan**\n\nStepping off the plane in Tokyo felt like entering a different dimension. The air was crisp, and the seamless blend of ancient tradition and futuristic technology was immediately striking. Neon signs buzzed overhead in Shibuya, while just a train ride away, serene shrines offered a moment of quiet reflection.\n\nMy first stop was Asakusa. Walking through the Kaminarimon Gate, I was surrounded by the scent of incense and the sound of wooden geta clacking on stone paths. I tried my hand at writing a wish on an *ema* plaque at Sensō-ji Temple, hoping to return someday. The contrast between the bustling crowds and the spiritual calm was mesmerizing.\n\nNo trip to Japan is complete without tackling the food scene. From steaming bowls of rich tonkotsu ramen in narrow alleyways to delicate sushi at Tsukiji Outer Market, every meal was an adventure. I even worked up the courage to try *natto* for breakfast—a fermented soybean dish that is definitely an acquired taste!\n\nMidweek, I escaped the city for Kyoto. Standing before the vermilion torii gates of Fushimi Inari Taisha, hiking up the mountain path felt like stepping into a fairy tale. The cherry blossoms were just beginning to bloom, painting the landscape in soft pinks and whites. Sitting by the Kamo River at sunset, watching locals picnic and play music, I felt a profound sense of peace.\n\nJapan captivates you not just with its sights, but with its hospitality (*omotenashi*) and attention to detail. It's a place where the past and future coexist harmoniously. As I boarded my flight home, I knew this wasn't a goodbye, but rather a \"see you later.\" Until next time, *sayonara*, Japan!", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.041201945}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.025032455}, {"token": "erry", "logprob": -0.055845086}, {"token": " Bloss", "logpr